<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Partial Info.plist for the watchOS app, merged with the keys Xcode generates from the
	  target's INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays YES) — the same
	  arrangement the iOS target uses, and for the same reason.

	  Read Config/RunExporter-Info.plist first: it explains why array-valued keys CANNOT be set
	  via INFOPLIST_KEY_*. Xcode accepts such a setting without error and silently emits no key.
	  That has cost this project real time twice, so verify every key below against the BUILT
	  product, never against -showBuildSettings:

	      plutil -extract UIBackgroundModes json -o - \
	        "./build/Build/Products/Release-iphoneos/RunExporter.app/Watch/RunExporterWatch Watch App.app/Info.plist"
	-->

	<!--
	  Identifies the iOS app this watch app belongs to. THIS KEY IS THE COMPANION
	  RELATIONSHIP — without it the app is a standalone watch app that merely happens to be
	  built alongside RunExporter.

	  Both mechanisms the one-tap feature depends on are companion-only:
	    * HKHealthStore.startWatchApp(toHandle:) launches "the WatchKit app corresponding to
	      the calling iOS application";
	    * WCSession pairs an iOS app with *its* watch app.
	  Neither has a counterpart to find if this is wrong or absent, and neither reports the
	  absence as a pairing problem — it looks like a connectivity bug several stages later.

	  Must match the iOS target's PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER exactly, and this app's own bundle
	  id must be that value + ".watchkitapp".
	-->
	<key>WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>is.doug.runexporter</string>

	<!--
	  "workout-processing" is what grants a watchOS app real background execution while an
	  HKWorkoutSession is running — the property the whole watch recorder rests on (plan §9,
	  stage 2: "survives 5 minutes with the wrist down and screen off").

	  "audio" is carried for parity with the phone. NOTE that cues deliberately stay on the
	  iPhone (plan §7a): AirPods play from one source at a time, and with media on the phone a
	  watch-side cue is never heard. This key is here so the option is not closed off, not
	  because watch audio is planned.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>workout-processing</string>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  WKBackgroundModes is watchOS's OWN background-modes key, separate from UIBackgroundModes
	  above. Added 2026-09-29. Evidence it exists and carries workout-processing: WatchKit exports
	  _SPInfoPlistWKBackgroundModesKey and _SPInfoPlistWKBackgroundModesWorkoutProcessingValue, and
	  Xcode's watch Background Modes capability (BACKGROUND_MODES_WATCH) writes this key.

	  Why it matters: the phone's HKHealthStore.startWatchApp(toHandle:) reached the Watch (phone
	  healthd logged the request and the Watch's reply), yet watchOS never launched this app — the
	  saved event log showed no process start and no handle(_:). This plist had workout-processing
	  only under UIBackgroundModes, written by hand on 2026-08-08.

	  MEASURED 2026-09-29 (LEARNINGS.md, "A phone launch needs WKBackgroundModes"): adding this key
	  was the only change in the next build, and that build launched. Kept alongside
	  UIBackgroundModes rather than replacing it, because the stage 2 probe passed with that key and
	  nothing that works should change in the same step.
	-->
	<key>WKBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>workout-processing</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  HealthKit read AND write, for THIS TARGET ONLY.

	  The iOS app never writes. Its export requests `toShare: []` (HealthKitManager.swift); since
	  2026-09-25 its watch link (WatchLink.swift) also asks for workout share access so the phone
	  can start this app's workout. Saving stays here, on the watch.

	  Write was approved deliberately (plan §6, decided 2026-08-08) because a recorder must call
	  HKLiveWorkoutBuilder.finishWorkout(), which saves an HKWorkout — there is no read-only way
	  to record a workout. Scope is as narrow as the API allows: **workouts and their GPS routes**
	  (HKSeriesType.workoutRoute, added 2026-09-29), requested from the watch app only. Do not
	  widen this to other sample types without revisiting the decision and the README's Privacy
	  section in the same change.

	  These strings are shown in the Health permission sheet. They are not a request: nothing is
	  written until code calls requestAuthorization(toShare:read:) and the user grants it.
	-->
	<!--
	  Location, for the GPS route saved with each workout (watch plan step 2, 2026-09-29). Only
	  while a workout runs; when-in-use, never always. The route is saved into Apple Health with the
	  workout, the same as Apple's own Workout app does, and the phone's export already reads it.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>RunExporter records your route during a workout, so the run is saved to Health with its map, the same as Apple's Workout app.</string>

	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>RunExporter reads your workout data on the Watch so it can record a run and keep the interval timer in sync with it.</string>

	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>RunExporter saves the run it records on your Watch to the Health app, so the workout appears alongside your other activity. It only ever writes workouts and their routes.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
